.TH FY\-MERMAID 1 "2026" "libfymermaid" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fy\-mermaid \- render mermaid diagrams on the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fy\-mermaid
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIfile\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fy\-mermaid
parses mermaid diagram sources and draws them with box drawing characters
and ANSI colour, sized for a terminal rather than for a browser.
With no \fIfile\fR, or with \fB\-\fR, the diagram is read from standard input.
.PP
YAML frontmatter and \fB%%{init: ...}%%\fR directives are both read, and are
applied in that order, so a directive overrides the frontmatter.
.SH DIAGRAM TYPES
.TP
.B flowchart
also spelled \fBgraph\fR; ranked \fBTB\fR or \fBLR\fR
.TP
.B classDiagram
boxed compartments with UML relation ends
.TP
.B sequenceDiagram
participant columns, arrows, notes and blocks
.TP
.B stateDiagram
also \fBstateDiagram\-v2\fR; composite states and pseudo\-states
.TP
.B gitGraph
branch lanes and commit edges
.TP
.B erDiagram
attribute compartments and crow's foot ends
.TP
.B xychart
also \fBxychart\-beta\fR; bars and stepped lines
.TP
.B usecase\-beta
actors, use cases and system boundaries
.TP
.B kanban
columns of cards
.TP
.B mindmap
an indented tree
.TP
.BR C4Context ", " C4Container ", " C4Component ", " C4Dynamic ", " C4Deployment
ranked boxes
.TP
.B block
also \fBblock\-beta\fR; a grid of blocks, with spans
.TP
.B quadrantChart
points plotted on a divided square
.TP
.B architecture\-beta
services ranked, groups as headings
.TP
.B packet
also \fBpacket\-beta\fR; bit fields in rows of thirty\-two
.TP
.B pie
proportional bars
.TP
.B timeline
sections and events, down the page
.TP
.B radar\-beta
bars grouped by axis
.TP
.B journey
tasks with their scores as pips
.TP
.B gantt
day\-resolved bars, honouring \fBafter\fR and \fBuntil\fR
.TP
.B agentflow\-beta
the flowchart grammar with flows, connectors and agent shapes
.TP
.BR railroad\-beta ", " railroad\-ebnf\-beta ", " railroad\-peg\-beta ", " railroad\-abnf\-beta
a grammar drawn as rails, in any of four notations
.TP
.B wardley\-beta
evolution across, visibility up, names in a legend
.TP
.B eventmodeling
frames in the column their kind belongs to
.TP
.B treeView\-beta
an indentation tree, with comments beside entries
.TP
.B ishikawa\-beta
causes drawn as ribs off a spine
.TP
.B info
the library version
.PP
A header naming any other diagram type is reported as unsupported.
.SH SPACING
The gaps a diagram is drawn with, the margin around it and the limits on its
size are library settings, reachable through \fBfymm_metrics_default\fR(3) and
\fBfymm_render_cfg\fR.  This tool exposes only the width and the fit policy;
a program that links the library can change the rest.
.SH RENDERING NOTES
Several mermaid layouts do not survive in character cells, and are drawn
differently rather than badly:
.IP \(bu 2
A pie becomes a sorted bar chart and a radar becomes bars grouped by axis.
Two angles a few percent apart cannot be compared by eye at this resolution;
two bars can.
.IP \(bu 2
A Wardley map numbers its nodes on the plot and names them in a legend
beneath it, because the names cannot sit on the plot without colliding.
.IP \(bu 2
A timeline runs down the page rather than across it, because a terminal is
tall and task text is prose.
.IP \(bu 2
A flowchart subgraph and an agentflow flow are drawn as a dashed frame around
the nodes they hold, with the title on the top edge. A C4 boundary and an
architecture group become a heading over their members instead: those nest
boxes that carry their own borders, and a frame around them leaves neither
legible.
.IP \(bu 2
A flowchart is drawn in the direction it asks for, \fBTB\fR or \fBLR\fR.
\fBBT\fR and \fBRL\fR reverse an axis the renderer does not reverse, and are
drawn as \fBTB\fR and \fBLR\fR with a warning.  A gitGraph is drawn left to
right whatever its header says.
.SH LABELS
A \fB<br>\fR breaks a label, in each spelling mermaid accepts.  Where the
diagram gives the label a box it becomes a second line; where the label is one
row of a list it is joined with a space.
.PP
A label written in backticks inside its quotes is a mermaid markdown string
and is formatted: \fB**bold**\fR, \fB_italic_\fR, \fB~~struck~~\fR, code and
links each reach the terminal as its own attribute.  A plain label is never
formatted, because a label may contain an asterisk.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIFILE\fR"
Write to \fIFILE\fR instead of standard output.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-width " \fIN\fR"
Render for a terminal \fIN\fR columns wide.  The default is taken from
\fB$COLUMNS\fR, then from the terminal itself, then 80.  The width is a
limit: no line of the output goes over it unless \fB\-\-fit none\fR says so.
.TP
.BR \-F ", " \-\-fit " \fIPOLICY\fR"
What to do with a diagram wider than the width.
.RS
.TP
.B shrink
Close the gaps between the parts of the drawing until it fits, then clip
whatever is still over.  This keeps the whole diagram on the screen for as
long as closing it up can.  An edge label that no longer fits the gap it sits
in is cut and ends in an ellipsis.  The default.
.TP
.B legend
Close the gaps as \fBshrink\fR does, and move a label that still does not fit
into a legend beneath the drawing, leaving a numbered marker in its place.
The marker takes the colour of its legend entry, so the eye finds the text
without counting.  A narrow terminal then loses the place of a label rather
than the label itself.  Only \fBgitGraph\fR does this so far; every other
type behaves as \fBshrink\fR.
.TP
.B clip
Draw at the natural size and clip to the width.  What fits is drawn exactly as
it would be with no limit, and the rest is lost off the right.
.TP
.B none
Ignore the width and emit the whole drawing, however wide.  For a pager that
scrolls sideways, or a file.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-color " \fIMODE\fR"
One of \fBauto\fR (the default), \fBnone\fR, \fB16\fR, \fB256\fR or \fBtrue\fR.
\fBauto\fR honours \fB$NO_COLOR\fR, \fB$CLICOLOR_FORCE\fR, \fB$COLORTERM\fR and
\fB$TERM\fR, and emits nothing when the output is not a terminal.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-charset " \fISET\fR"
One of \fBauto\fR (the default), \fBascii\fR, \fBunicode\fR or \fBrich\fR.
\fBauto\fR reads the locale and settles on \fBunicode\fR or \fBascii\fR.
.sp
\fBrich\fR adds what asks more of the font than the box drawing does: a line
series is plotted on braille, at twice the horizontal and four times the
vertical resolution of the cells, and a decision takes the heavy border weight
so that it stops looking like a gateway.  \fBauto\fR never selects it, because
the locale says the terminal speaks UTF\-8 and not which glyphs its font
carries.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-ascii
Shorthand for \fB\-\-charset ascii\fR.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-theme " \fINAME\fR"
Colour the diagram with a built-in theme.  \fB\-\-list\-themes\fR names them.
The theme is applied over the built-in palette, so a theme that sets one
colour keeps the rest.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-style " \fIFILE\fR"
Read a theme file, applied over \fB\-\-theme\fR.  The file is YAML with a
\fBcolors\fR mapping keyed by \fBgit0\fR to \fBgit7\fR, \fBcommitLabel\fR,
\fBtag\fR and \fBtitle\fR.  A value is \fB#rgb\fR, \fB#rrggbb\fR, an xterm
palette index, or an ANSI colour name; it may instead be a mapping carrying
\fBcolor\fR, \fBbold\fR and \fBdim\fR.  A colour is reduced to the nearest
entry of the palette the terminal can take.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-list\-themes
List the built-in themes and exit.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-background " \fIMODE\fR"
One of \fBauto\fR (the default), \fBdark\fR or \fBlight\fR.  \fBauto\fR reads
\fB$FYMM_BACKGROUND\fR, then \fB$COLORFGBG\fR, and then asks the terminal with
an OSC 11 query.  A light terminal takes the \fBlight\fR theme unless
\fB\-\-theme\fR already named one.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Draw the diagram and move a selection over it.  The arrow keys and
.BR h ", " j ", " k " and " l
move the selection to the nearest element in that direction,
.B tab
and
.B shift\-tab
walk every element in turn, and a mouse click selects the element under the
pointer.
.B Enter
leaves the viewer and prints the path of the selected element;
.B q
leaves without printing one.
It takes one file, needs a terminal on both standard input and standard
output, and cannot be combined with \fB\-\-output\fR or \fB\-\-dump\-model\fR.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-dump\-model
Emit the parsed diagram model as YAML instead of rendering it.  The model is
the same
.BR fy_generic
value the library hands to a caller, and is the stable thing to test against.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-flow
With \fB\-\-dump\-model\fR, emit flow style rather than block style.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-strict
Treat warnings as errors.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Do not report diagnostics.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the library version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage message and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero when every input parsed and rendered, one otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fy-tool (1)
